--TEST--
`push` refuses a `grants:` naming a capability the enqueuing request does not hold, and it does so before it reaches the database — `rule:concurrency/a-jobs-budget-and-grants-are-recorded-at-enqueue`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// A job's grants are narrowed from the request that enqueued it and never
// widened, so a name that request cannot ask for itself is not a narrowing of
// anything: recording it would hand background work authority the request in
// front of it never had, which is what makes a queue a privilege-escalation
// seam. Nothing here grants `net.connect`, so nothing here may enqueue work
// that reaches the network.
//
// The block below names a host nothing is listening on, which is the second
// assertion this case makes: if `push` reached the connection before it judged
// its own arguments, this would be an `IOError` about a refused socket rather
// than the refusal about the argument, and it would say something different on
// a machine that happened to have a server there.
try {
    var $id = Core\Queue::push("jobs/send-receipt.nvs", {grants: ["net.connect"]});
    echo "enqueued", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo $refused->message, "\n";
}

echo "still running", "\n";
--FILE nvs.toml--
# A queue whose connection resolves at boot -- `connection` names a block that
# exists, which is all `nvs_config::queue` checks -- and whose port has nothing
# behind it. Reaching it would be the failure this case is written to rule out.
# No `[capabilities]` table at all, which is the deployment granting nothing.
[queue]
connection = "main"

[db.main]
driver = "postgres"
host = "127.0.0.1"
port = 1
--EXPECT--
Core\Queue::push: `grants` names `net.connect`, which this request does not hold — a job's grants are narrowed from the request that enqueued it and never widened
help: grant it in nvs.toml under `[capabilities.net]`, or drop it from `grants`
still running
